[{"record_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121","candidate_id":"june_update_2026-06-10__rec_000121","source_record_id":"rec_000121","source_corpus":"include","title":"RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning","abstract":"Integrating continuous single-cell gene expression profiles with discrete-token large language models (LLMs) remains an open challenge: text-based methods are token-inefficient and discard quantitative precision, continuous embeddings preclude autoregressive generation, and single-codebook vector quantization cannot stratify biological scales.\n\nWe present RVQ-Alpha , an end-to-end framework that bridges this modality gap through three contributions:\n\n(i) a Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ) tokenizer that compresses each cell into a fixed 10-token sequence via eight residual codebooks embedded directly in the LLM vocabulary, requiring 3.4 × fewer tokens than prior discrete methods and enabling bidirectional unification of cell interpretation and generation;\n\n(ii) scCoT-Synth , a teacher-student engine that grounds newly added biological tokens through evidence-before-conclusion reasoning, using the language modeling objective as the cross-modal alignment signal without a separate projection network;\n\nand (iii) a Fact-Aware RLVR system combining an ontology-grounded answer judge with saliency-weighted verification of biological claims against actual expression data, under dynamic gating that conditions hallucination suppression on task competence.\n\nBuilt on Qwen3-4B and trained via continued pretraining, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning, RVQ-Alpha substantially improves out-of-distribution generalization and rare-cell recognition across eight held-out datasets; [truncated]","doi":"10.64898/2026.04.20.719773","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nIntegrating continuous single-cell gene expression profiles with discrete-token large language models (LLMs) remains an open challenge: text-based methods are token-inefficient and discard quantitative precision, continuous embeddings preclude autoregressive generation, and single-codebook vector quantization cannot stratify biological scales.\n\nWe present RVQ-Alpha , an end-to-end framework that bridges this modality gap through three contributions:\n\n(i) a Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ) tokenizer that compresses each cell into a fixed 10-token sequence via eight residual codebooks embedded directly in the LLM vocabulary, requiring 3.4 × fewer tokens than prior discrete methods and enabling bidirectional unification of cell interpretation and generation;\n\n(ii) scCoT-Synth , a teacher-student engine that grounds newly added biological tokens through evidence-before-conclusion reasoning, using the language modeling objective as the cross-modal alignment signal without a separate projection network;\n\nand (iii) a Fact-Aware RLVR system combining an ontology-grounded answer judge with saliency-weighted verification of biological claims against actual expression data, under dynamic gating that conditions hallucination suppression on task competence.\n\nBuilt on Qwen3-4B and trained via continued pretraining, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning, RVQ-Alpha substantially improves out-of-distribution generalization and rare-cell recognition across eight held-out datasets; [truncated]\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nBuilding computational models that understand and simulate cellular behavior, a vision termed the AI Virtual Cell [1], is a central goal of computational biology. Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) [2] provides the high-resolution transcriptomic data required by such models, and large language models (LLMs) offer powerful reasoning and generation capabilities that could serve as their computational substrate [3, 4]. Realizing this vision demands a model that can both interpret existing cell states (e.g., cell type annotation, disease classification) and generate new cell states (e.g., perturbation simulation) within a unified architecture. We address a specific setting: compressing continuous gene expression profiles into discrete tokens that reside directly in the LLM vocabulary, enabling a single autoregressive model to achieve both the understanding and generation of cellular representations.\n\nThis problem is particularly challenging because it requires simultaneously preserving quantitative expression information, achieving token efficiency, and enabling autoregressive generation through the LLM vocabulary.\n\nSingle-cell foundation models have scaled rapidly (from scGPT [5] with 33M cells and Geneformer [6] with 30M cells, to CellFM [7] with 100M cells and 800M parameters, and scPRINT-2 [8] with 350M cells across 16 organisms); yet, systematic benchmarks show that no single model dominates across all downstream tasks (Tables 8 and 9) [9, 10, 11]. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nWe presented RVQ-Alpha, an end-to-end framework that bridges single-cell transcriptomics and large language models through three key components: RVQ discrete tokenization, Evidence-First FeatureInjection SFT, and RLVR with fact verification. By converting continuous gene expression profiles into discrete tokens and embedding them directly into the LLM vocabulary, RVQ-Alpha achieves bidirectional unification, enabling both the interpretation and generation of cell representations within a single model.\n\nEmpirical results demonstrate that RLVR with domain-specific verifiable rewards yields a 4.6 × performance improvement on population classification, a 28.9 pp increase in accuracy for rare cell types, and a 12.1 pp gain in out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization (Tables 8 and 9). The fact verification module is critical for grounding model outputs in actual expression data, thereby reducing hallucinations and enhancing generalization (Table 10).\n\nRVQ-Alpha establishes a foundation for AI Virtual Cells, providing a scalable, interpretable, and generative substrate for multi-modal biological intelligence. Future work will extend this framework to support cross-species generalization, atlas-scale populations, and the integration of additional modalities (e.g., spatial transcriptomics and chromatin accessibility).\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\n### References\n\n- [1] Charlotte Bunne, Yusuf Roohani, Yanay Rosen, et al. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Datasets]\nCorpus identity. We draw pretraining and supervised fine-tuning data from the ScBank repository of donor-level human scRNA-seq profiles.\n\nOur working corpus is a 55-file indexed donor-level subset comprising 31,481,661 cells from approximately 45 source study families (the provenance index is released with the repository).\n\nThe indexed corpus spans 101 standardized tissues , 103 disease states , and 859 standardized cell-type labels (Table 5).\n\nThese counts describe the harmonized metadata space of the corpus and exceed the effective class counts of any individual benchmark task (§4.4).\n\nOf the 55 dataset IDs, 53 are traceable to upstream ScBank source records; the remaining two are documented in our corpus index without a corresponding catalog match.\n\nMetadata harmonization. Tissue, disease, and cell-type annotations were canonicalized through fieldalias normalization and dictionary-based value mapping before any downstream task construction.\n\nBecause metadata completeness varies across files (for instance, the donor identifier and tissue are present in all 55 files, whereas disease, cell type, sex, and age coverage is partial, as shown in Table 5), RVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 5: Corpus summary: 55-file indexed subset of ScBank. Label counts reflect the harmonized metadata space of the indexed corpus, not the effective class counts of any downstream benchmark (cf. Table 6). † [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Supplementary G: LCAD Resolution Cascade and Per-Dataset Coverage]\nThis subsection expands the deterministic five-tier name resolution cascade summarized in §4.5 and reports the per-dataset coverage statistics referenced from §4.7. Because model outputs and groundtruth labels use heterogeneous naming conventions (e.g., 'Treg' vs. 'regulatory T cell', 'CD8+ T cell' vs. 'CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell'), the cascade resolves names to CL identifiers before any graph distance is computed.\n\n1. Tier 0 - Exact match : if the predicted and ground-truth strings are identical, LCAD = 0.\n2. Tier 1 - Direct Cell Ontology lookup : both names are looked up in the name-to-identifier index built from the OBO Foundry Cell Ontology release ( cl.obo ) recorded in §4.5; on success, LCAD is computed by finding the lowest common ancestor on the is-a DAGand summing the shortest-path distances from each node to that ancestor.\n3. Tier 2 - Synonym mapping : a human-curated synonym table maps common variants (e.g., 'Treg' → 'regulatory T cell') to canonical names, then retries Tier 1.\n4. Tier 3 - Deterministic normalization : a deterministic string-rewriting normalizer applies six rule groups-CD-marker normalization, Greek-letter expansion, tissue/layer prefix stripping, '-like' suffix removal, dataset-code removal, and whitespace collapse-and re-attempts Tier 1.\n5. Tier 4 - Semantic-relation fallback : when Tiers 0-3 all fail, the semantic-relation label assigned by the post-training evaluation judge is mapped through a static lookup table whose maximum value is 6.0. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Supplementary H: Per-Dataset Cross-System Accuracy]\nTable 16 reports the per-dataset cell type accuracy for all four systems evaluated on the 8-dataset OOD split. The corresponding bar chart visualisation appears in Figure 5 in the main text.\n\navailable under a CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.04.20.719773; this version posted April 23, 2026. The copyright holder for this preprint\n\nRVQ-Alpha: Bridging Single-Cell Transcriptomics and Large Language Models via Discrete Tokenization and Verifiable Reinforcement Learning\n\nTable 16: Cross-system comparison on the 8-dataset OOD split. Dataset identifiers (A013, D094-D103) are the ScBank IDs introduced in §4.4. Best accuracy per dataset in bold . All models use pass@8 (8 stochastic decodings, best-of-8).\n\n| Dataset | RVQ-Alpha (pass@8) | LangCell (pass@8) | Cell-o1 (pass@8) | C2S-Gemma2-2B (pass@8) |\n|-----------|----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|--------------------------|\n| A013 | 49.4% | 13.2% | 23.1% | 27.8% |\n| D094 | 35.0% | 15.8% | 37.4% | 24.6% |\n| D095 | 80.9% | 24.5% | 28.3% | 56.1% |\n| D096 | 54.4% | 27.2% | 18.7% | 41.3% |\n| D099 | 45.1% | 11.3% | 15.9% | 64.3% |\n| D101 | 34.2% | 8.7% | 12.4% | 34.7% |\n| D102 | 47.8% | 19.6% | 22.1% | 35.2% |\n| D103 | 74.7% | 4.1% | 9.8% | 96.0% |\n\n##### Cell Type Homogeneous: Cross-Lineage\n\nFigure 15: Per-cell-type accuracy distribution - cell type classification. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Supplementary E: RVQ Tokenizer Architecture, Training, and Vocabulary Expansion]\nThis supplementary consolidates the implementation details abstracted away in §3.2: the deployed architecture configuration, the full training objective, the augmentation curriculum, the embeddinginitialization procedure, and the vocabulary-expansion mechanics.\n\nArchitecture configuration. Table 14 lists the hyperparameters of the deployed RVQ autoencoder, matching the values released alongside the public checkpoint.\n\nTable 14: RVQ tokenizer architecture configuration. All values correspond to the released model. The encoder is a two-layer MLP with hidden widths 1024 and 512 mapping G → 128; each linear layer is followed by batch normalisation and a leaky ReLU activation. The decoder mirrors this architecture (128 → 512 → 1024 → G ).\n\n| Parameter | Description | Value |\n|---------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|-------------|\n| Input dimensionality ( G ) | Number of measured genes | 36,601 |\n| Hidden dimensionalities | Encoder/decoder hidden layer sizes | [1024, 512] |\n| Latent dimensionality ( D ) | Latent vector dimensionality | 128 |\n| Number of codebooks ( N c ) | Number of RVQ codebook levels | 8 |\n| Codebook size ( C ) | Entries per codebook | 32 |\n| Quantization weight ( λ q ) | Quantization loss weight | 0.1 |\n| Total RVQ vocabulary tokens ( N c × C ) | Total RVQ vocabulary tokens ( N c × C ) | 256 |\n| Information rate per cell ( N c · log 2 C ) | Information rate per cell ( N c · log 2 C ) | 40 bits |\n| Encoder parameters | Encoder parameters | ∼ 38.1M |\n| Decoder parameters | Decoder parameters [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":4,"line_start":7,"line_end":26,"text":"Integrating continuous single-cell gene expression profiles with discrete-token large language models (LLMs) remains an open challenge: text-based methods are token-inefficient and discard quantitative precision, continuous embeddings preclude autoregressive generation, and single-codebook vector quantization cannot stratify biological scales.\n\nWe present RVQ-Alpha , an end-to-end framework that bridges this modality gap through three contributions:\n\n(i) a Residual Vector Quantization (RVQ) tokenizer that compresses each cell into a fixed 10-token sequence via eight residual codebooks embedded directly in the LLM vocabulary, requiring 3.4 × fewer tokens than prior 